---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="live-data">

  <h2 id="problem">Many small changes</h2>
  <p>
    A ticker, a monitoring view, a trading screen: values change many times per second, but the rows stay the same. Rebuilding
    the table – or even repainting all visible cells – for every change would waste most of the frame.
  </p>

  <h2 id="update-cells">updateCells: value + redraw of exactly these cells</h2>
  <Snippet file="demos/live-data.ts" region="model" title="src/model/ticker.ts" />
  <Snippet file="demos/live-data.ts" region="tick" />
  <Snippet file="demos/live-data.ts" region="timer" />
  <ul>
    <li>A <ApiLink name="TableCellUpdateEvent" /> = area, row, column, new value, and optionally CSS classes for this cell.</li>
    <li><ApiLink name="TableApi.updateCells" /> writes the values into the model and redraws only the affected cells – if they are
      visible. Cells off screen just get the value and are drawn when you scroll there.</li>
    <li><code>updateCells(events, true)</code> repaints the whole table instead.</li>
  </ul>
  <LiveDemo name="live-data" title="1,000 quotes, 1,500 updates per second" height={420}
            caption="Green = up, pink = down. Scroll while it runs – the frame rate stays at 60 fps." />

  <Callout type="note" title="Flash, don't paint">
    <p>The classes of the event apply to the <strong>current</strong> drawing of the cell – a flash. After the next full repaint
      they are gone. Colours that must stay belong into the model (<ApiLink name="AreaModelIf.getCustomClassesAt" />,
      <ChapterLink slug="heatmap" />).</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="version">
    <p>Since 1.1.89 <code>updateCells()</code> only searches the cells of its own table – before, several tables on one page could
      update each other's cells.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="sources">Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    WebSocket, Server-Sent Events, polling – collect the changes and call <code>updateCells</code> once per animation frame or
    interval, not once per message. The showcase <a href="/showcase/crypto/run/">Crypto</a> does that with real price data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="overlays">Loading and empty tables</h2>
  <p>
    Before the first data arrive, the body is empty – and an empty table looks broken. The table has three overlays for
    that, plain HTML and CSS (the spinner is a CSS animation, no image, no library):
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/overlays.ts" region="table" />
  <Snippet file="demos/overlays.ts" region="load" />
  <ul>
    <li><ApiLink name="TableApi.showLoadingOverlay" /> shows <em>Loading…</em> over the body until
      <ApiLink name="TableApi.hideOverlay" />. Rows already shown stay visible under a semi-transparent layer; mouse and
      editing are blocked meanwhile (<code>blockInteractionWhileLoading</code>).</li>
    <li><em>No rows to show</em> appears by itself when the body is empty, <em>No matching rows</em> when the filter hides
      every row – <code>hideOverlay()</code> does not end them, they describe the body. <ApiLink name="TableApi.getOverlayState" />
      tells which one is shown.</li>
    <li>Texts, a delay against flashing (<code>loadingDelayInMs</code>) and own content: <ApiLink name="OverlayOptionsIf" />.
      Screen readers hear the texts; the table is <code>aria-busy</code> while loading.</li>
  </ul>
  <LiveDemo name="overlays" title="Loading, empty answer, no matches" height={360}
            caption="Click “refresh”: the spinner lies over the rows. “empty answer” and a search for “zzz” show the empty states." />
  <Callout type="version">
    <p>Since 1.1.91. The framework wrappers have a <code>loading</code> prop – <code>&lt;GuiexpertTable loading=&#123;isLoading&#125; … /&gt;</code> –
      that shows the overlay without re-creating the table.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>A table that remembers sorting and scroll position: <ChapterLink slug="persist-state" />.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
